class AuditService:
    def __init__(self):
        self._audit = []
    def log(self, message, level="info"):
        entry = f"[{level}] {message}"
        self._audit.append(entry)
        return entry
    def history(self):
        return list(self._audit)
    ...
```

```python
class NotificationService:
    def __init__(self, notifier):
        self._notifier = notifier
        self._sent = 0
    def notify(self, customer, subject):
        result = self._notifier.send(customer.email(), subject)
        self._sent += 1
        return result
    def sent_count(self):
        return self._sent
    def reset_sent(self):
        prev = self._sent
        self._sent = 0
        return prev
```

OrderService becomes a thin orchestrator that delegates to these services. Each new class has a focused responsibility, reducing internal cohesion issues and lowering method counts and complexity per class.

